  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "False positives and rapid tests put the rate of infection near zero.",
      "sourceText": "False positives and rapid tests put the rate of infection near zero",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 67,
      "role": "NUMERIC_CONSTRAINT"
    },
    {
      "id": "atom-2",
      "text": "There will be no second wave of infections.",
      "sourceText": "there will be no second wave of infections",
      "start": 77,
      "end": 119,
      "role": "CAUSAL_RELATION"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-6",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Evidence 2: Are the rate of false positives in rapid covid tests high or low? ",
          "start": 343,
          "end": 421,
          "context": "Yes\n Evidence 2: Are the rate of false positives in rapid covid tests high or low?  Tests only gave false positive results in 2% of those without COVID-19.\n\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-21",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Failure of diagnostic tests to detect infection with COVID-19 when it is present (a false negative result) may delay treatment and risk further spread of infection to others. ",
          "start": 1811,
          "end": 1986,
          "context": "Accurate testing allows identification of people who need to isolate themselves to prevent the spread of infection, or who might need treatment for their infection.  Failure of diagnostic tests to detect infection with COVID-19 when it is present (a false negative result) may delay treatment and risk further spread of infection to others.  Incorrect diagnosis of COVID-19 when it is not present (a false positive result) may lead to unnecessary further testing, treatment, and isolation of the person and close contacts. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Evidence 1: Do Covid rapid tests show any false positive results? ",
          "start": 273,
          "end": 339,
          "context": "Source URL: https://www.cochrane.org/CD013652/INFECTN_what-diagnostic-accuracy-antibody-tests-detection-infection-covid-19-virus\n Evidence 1: Do Covid rapid tests show any false positive results?  Yes\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-126",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "In a seroprevalence survey, where prevalence of prior infection is 50%, we would anticipate that 51 (46 to 58) cases would be missed and 6 (5 to 7) would be falsely positive in 1000 people having IgG tests during the convalescent phase (21 to 100 days post-symptom onset or post-positive PCR) of SARS-CoV-2 infection.",
          "start": 16446,
          "end": 16763,
          "context": "In a low-prevalence (2%) setting, where antibody testing is used to diagnose COVID-19 in people with symptoms but who have had a negative PCR test, we would anticipate that 1 (1 to 2) case would be missed and 8 (5 to 15) would be falsely positive in 1000 people undergoing IgG or IgM testing in week three after onset of SARS-CoV-2 infection.\n In a seroprevalence survey, where prevalence of prior infection is 50%, we would anticipate that 51 (46 to 58) cases would be missed and 6 (5 to 7) would be falsely positive in 1000 people having IgG tests during the convalescent phase (21 to 100 days post-symptom onset or post-positive PCR) of SARS-CoV-2 infection."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-36",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "We looked for studies that measured the diagnostic accuracy of antibody tests to detect current or past COVID-19 infection and compared them with reference standard criteria. ",
          "start": 3651,
          "end": 3826,
          "context": "What did we do?\n We looked for studies that measured the diagnostic accuracy of antibody tests to detect current or past COVID-19 infection and compared them with reference standard criteria.  Since there are many antibody tests available, we included studies assessing any antibody test compared with any reference standard. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-22",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Incorrect diagnosis of COVID-19 when it is not present (a false positive result) may lead to unnecessary further testing, treatment, and isolation of the person and close contacts. ",
          "start": 1986,
          "end": 2167,
          "context": "Failure of diagnostic tests to detect infection with COVID-19 when it is present (a false negative result) may delay treatment and risk further spread of infection to others.  Incorrect diagnosis of COVID-19 when it is not present (a false positive result) may lead to unnecessary further testing, treatment, and isolation of the person and close contacts.  Accurate identification of people who have previously had COVID-19 is important in measuring disease spread and assessing the success of public health interventions.\n"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "atomId": "atom-2",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd",
          "text": "Evidence 1: After the claim date was a second wave of Covid infection expected? ",
          "start": 243,
          "end": 323,
          "context": "Source URL: https://www.webmd.com/lung/news/20200925/us-still-in-first-wave-of-covid-19-fauci-says\n Evidence 1: After the claim date was a second wave of Covid infection expected?  Yes\n\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd::sentence-7",
          "documentId": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd",
          "text": "Sept. 25, 2020 -- Anthony Fauci, MD, says talk about a second wave of the coronavirus is premature because the United States is still dealing with the first one.\n",
          "start": 350,
          "end": 512,
          "context": "RECOVERED SOURCE TEXT\n Sept. 25, 2020 -- Anthony Fauci, MD, says talk about a second wave of the coronavirus is premature because the United States is still dealing with the first one.\n The idea of a second wave is based on the 1918 flu pandemic, when many cases were seen in the spring, he says. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd::sentence-10",
          "documentId": "doc-f5cfb7b8dfcd",
          "text": "“Rather than say, ‘A second wave,’ why don’t we say, ‘Are we prepared for the challenge of the fall and the winter?’” said Fauci, director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases and a member of the White House coronavirus task force.\n",
          "start": 820,
          "end": 1076,
          "context": "The spring cases \"literally disappeared\" and were followed by a spike in flu cases in the fall, he told CNN’s Sanjay Gupta, MD, on Thursday in an online conversation organized by Emory University.\n “Rather than say, ‘A second wave,’ why don’t we say, ‘Are we prepared for the challenge of the fall and the winter?’” said Fauci, director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases and a member of the White House coronavirus task force.\n Flu shots are an important measure to help the U.S. get through the winter, he said.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-48",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "In week 2 after first symptoms, 64% to 79% of infections were detected, rising to 78% to 88% in week 3. ",
          "start": 4949,
          "end": 5053,
          "context": "In participants that had COVID-19 and were tested one week after symptoms developed, antibody tests detected only 27% to 41% of infections.  In week 2 after first symptoms, 64% to 79% of infections were detected, rising to 78% to 88% in week 3.  Tests that specifically detected IgG or IgM antibodies were the most accurate and, when testing people from 21 days after first symptoms, they detected 93% of people with COVID-19. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-7",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "Tests only gave false positive results in 2% of those without COVID-19.\n\n",
          "start": 421,
          "end": 494,
          "context": "Evidence 2: Are the rate of false positives in rapid covid tests high or low?  Tests only gave false positive results in 2% of those without COVID-19.\n\n Antibody tests one week after first symptoms only detected 30% of people who had COVID-19. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-450521860e04::sentence-47",
          "documentId": "doc-450521860e04",
          "text": "In participants that had COVID-19 and were tested one week after symptoms developed, antibody tests detected only 27% to 41% of infections. ",
          "start": 4809,
          "end": 4949,
          "context": "Main results\n In participants that had COVID-19 and were tested one week after symptoms developed, antibody tests detected only 27% to 41% of infections.  In week 2 after first symptoms, 64% to 79% of infections were detected, rising to 78% to 88% in week 3. "
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
